#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129008 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/id #uuid "066f76be-83f6-52fd-ab3f-00786d18f64e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9866" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = t-\\sin t, \\quad y = 1-\\cos t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\pi\\right]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi y\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} y \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=\\pi$.\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t - \\sin t) = 1 - \\cos t \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1 - \\cos t) = \\sin t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}y\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{(1 - \\cos t)^{2} + (\\sin t)^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{1 - 2\\cos t + \\cos^{2} t + \\sin^{2} t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the identity\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 t +\\cos^2 t= 1\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{1 - 2\\cos t + (\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{1 - 2\\cos t + 1}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{2 - 2\\cos t}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t)\\sqrt{2(1 - \\cos t)}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{π}(1 - \\cos t) \\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{1 - \\cos t}dt \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}π∫_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{(1 - \\cos t)^{3}}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129009 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a9a59d4-e8c8-59ac-bf23-eb70254a7661" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/id #uuid "b7f404a3-8885-5a33-9cfb-1e12a6c745a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-9867" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = \\sin t, \\quad y = e^t, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1$.\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t) = \\cos t \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{t}) = e^{t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}x\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}\\sin t\\sqrt{(\\cos t)^{2} + (e^{t})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}\\sin t\\sqrt{\\cos^{2} t + e^{2t}}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129010 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7087df58-f97e-5423-813a-9aada16ee12a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Surface Area of Revolution"}] :question/id #uuid "205f4d6b-affa-530e-979b-a394e7a46bd3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9869" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = 1+3t, \\quad y = 2t^{3/2}, \\quad t\\in \\left[0,1\\right]$.\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} x \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from $t_1=0$ to $t_2=1$.\n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(1 + 3t) = 3 \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3/2}) = 3t^{1/2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 2π∫_{t_{1}}^{t_{2}}x\\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t)\\sqrt{(3)^{2} + (3t^{1/2})^{2}}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t)\\sqrt{9 + 9t}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t)\\sqrt{9(1 + t)}dt \\\\\n&= 2π∫_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t) \\cdot 3\\sqrt{1 + t}dt \\\\\n&= 6π∫_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t)\\sqrt{1 + t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution\n\n$$\nu = 1+t\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n1+3t=3u-2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{d}u = \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= 6π∫_{0}^{1}(1 + 3t)\\sqrt{1 + t}dt \\\\\n&= 6π∫_{1}^{2}(3u - 2)\\sqrt{u}du \\\\\n&= 6π∫_{1}^{2}(3u^{3/2} - 2u^{1/2})du \\\\\n&= 6π \\cdot [\\frac{6u^{5/2}}{5} - \\frac{4u^{3/2}}{3}]_{1}^{2} \\\\\n&= 6π[((6(2^{5/2}))/(5) - (4(2^{3/2}))/(3)) - (\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{4}{3})] \\\\\n&= 6π[(\\frac{24\\sqrt{2}}{5} - \\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{3}) - (\\frac{6}{5} - \\frac{4}{3})] \\\\\n&= 6π[\\frac{32\\sqrt{2}}{15} + \\frac{2}{15}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{4π}{5}(16\\sqrt{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d16c44c3-0f58-57b6-bbc3-003ce3d7916a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π∫_{0}^{π/4}\\cos^{2} t\\sin t\\sqrt{16\\cos^{2} t - 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126339} {:answer/id #uuid "0e1efc18-e010-553c-8f9e-6780a7db962b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/4}\\cos t\\sin^{2} t\\sqrt{9\\cos^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126340} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3cca44-c196-5525-9e7e-045dc8583ccf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/4}\\cos^{2} t\\sin t\\sqrt{8\\cos^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126341} {:answer/id #uuid "6d91462c-2690-56e5-aea0-a32058232638" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "π∫_{0}^{π/4}\\cos^{2} t\\sin t\\sqrt{9\\sin^{2} t - 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126342} {:answer/id #uuid "797c50af-4028-5fe2-b2e9-fe0fbf0b46cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/4}\\cos t\\sin t\\sqrt{16\\cos^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "797c50af-4028-5fe2-b2e9-fe0fbf0b46cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2π∫_{0}^{π/4}\\cos t\\sin t\\sqrt{16\\cos^{2} t + 1}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126343} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ef9768c-3daa-5487-b479-dda4d60c023e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126338}] :question/id #uuid "2fe9bcdf-6522-538a-a529-97fa9381c2b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138944" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$x = \\cos 2t, y = \\cos t, t ∈ [0, \\frac{π}{4}]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2π$ radians about the $x$-axis?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a9a59d4-e8c8-59ac-bf23-eb70254a7661" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfec902a-5cf6-5bd9-be1a-e3d96e022a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}π∫_{0}^{π}\\sin^{2} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126363} {:answer/id #uuid "f93032b9-b3fc-5327-adc8-0e6f60a52086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π∫_{0}^{π}\\cos^{2} tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126364} {:answer/id #uuid "3efaf6b4-0d42-576d-955e-71bfd02a85ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π∫_{0}^{π}\\cos tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126365} {:answer/id #uuid "5a771360-6985-5115-abec-e29fd81217f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π∫_{0}^{π}\\sin tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126366} {:answer/id #uuid "60a911b4-cf87-501e-9618-b1012b51b0c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}π∫_{0}^{π}\\sin t\\cos tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a771360-6985-5115-abec-e29fd81217f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π∫_{0}^{π}\\sin tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126366} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9753294-5be4-52de-ae49-8ba1ea6b80bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126362}] :question/id #uuid "67f3e12f-3cac-5a99-84d0-463e68f2be51" :question/math-academy-id "q-138981" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$x = 2\\sin t, y = 2\\cos t, t ∈ [0, π]$.\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2π$ radians about the $y$-axis?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129428 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b02082a8-b56e-5478-859d-51db7273f765" :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2508599-5947-55a7-8754-01f225e6e2d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π∫_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{e^{2t} + 16}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126375} {:answer/id #uuid "f6100d9e-739c-5220-b222-8d5c9a1f856d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16π∫_{0}^{2}te^{2t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126376} {:answer/id #uuid "43fcbbb3-c5bd-5570-a320-bcca8f5760b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π∫_{0}^{2}te^{t}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126377} {:answer/id #uuid "505ed0d9-e081-5b42-9ea5-6091bf73df8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π∫_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{e^{t} + 16t^{2}}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126378} {:answer/id #uuid "5e4853da-f898-544e-9a88-f3f0c060bf4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4π∫_{0}^{2}e^{t}\\sqrt{e^{t} + 4}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2508599-5947-55a7-8754-01f225e6e2d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8π∫_{0}^{2}t\\sqrt{e^{2t} + 16}dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126375} :answer-field/id #uuid "3993f0fe-ccec-5154-b1ee-20c011a3a669" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126374}] :question/id #uuid "c7215440-4058-5eea